 (2 points) plus possibly \u201cAll Types\u201d (6 points) and others.  \n\n---\n\n**3. Discard choice:**  \nWe need to discard a tile that is least useful for making melds, considering tile availability (unshown tiles count).  \n\nLet\u2019s check availability for key tiles:  \n- T3 (needed for T3\u2013T4\u2013T5): 3 left  \n- T6 (needed for T4\u2013T5\u2013T6): 3 left  \n- B3 (needed for B1\u2013B2\u2013B3): 4 left  \n- B7 (needed for B7\u2013B8\u2013B9): 4 left  \n\nAll relatively available.  \n\nWhat\u2019s less useful?  \n- **B4**: alone, no immediate partner except B3\u2013B4\u2013B5 or B4\u2013B5\u2013B6 (but B5 mostly gone? Unshown B5: 2 left, but across player has B4\u2013B5\u2013B6 meld, so B5 less available). B4 could be part of B2\u2013B3\u2013B4 if we get B3.  \n- **F4**: honor, only useful for pair/pung. Unshown F4: 3 left (total 4, 1 in hand, so 3 left). Could be good for pair.  \n- **J1**: unshown 4 left.  \n- **J3**: unshown 3 left.  \n\nIf we aim for \u201cAll Types\u201d, we need to keep at least one of each suit and honors.  \n\nWe have W in meld, T in hand (T4, T5), B in hand (several), F and J in hand.  \n\nSo discard candidates:  \n- **B4** is not helping much right now \u2014 B2 and B1 want B3, not B4. B8 and B9 want B7. So B4 is isolated.  \n- **B8 or B9** \u2014 but they work together for B7\u2013B8\u2013B9 Chow, so keep both.  \n- **W4** \u2014 we already have W1\u2013W3 meld, W4 not connected to it (except W2\u2013W3\u2013W4 but W2/W3 used). Useless.  \n- **T4 or T5** \u2014 we need these for T Chow, so keep.  \n\nBest discard seems **W4** or **B4**.  \n\n---\n\n**4. Defensive consideration:**  \nLooking at game history:  \n- Next player melds: W4\u2013W5\u2013W6 (Chow) and F1 pung. They discarded T4 earlier (so maybe safe).  \n- Across player has many T/B melds, discarded T1, W9, B5, etc.  \n- Prev player no melds.  \n\nW4: Next player already has W4\u2013W5\u2013W6, so W4 might be safe-ish (they completed sequence). But someone else might need W4 for pung? Unshown W4: 3 left.  \n\nB4: Across player has B4\u2013B5\u2013B6, so B4 might be safer (they don\u2019t need another B4). Unshown B4: 3 left.  \n\n---\n\n**5. Scoring potential:**  \nIf we keep all honors and three suits, we can aim for:  \nAll Types (6) + All Chows (2) + possibly Self-Drawn (1) = 9 points minimum. That\u2019s enough to win.  \n\nSo we must keep T4, T5, B1, B2, B8, B9, F4, J1, J3.  \nThat\u2019s 9 tiles. Plus meld W1\u2013W3.  \n\nFrom 9 hand tiles, we can discard B4 or W4 \u2014 both not needed for All Chows plan.  \n\n---\n\n**6. Final choice:**  \nB4 is slightly more dangerous? B4 is middle tile, still 3 left, but across player may need B4 for pung? Not likely, they have B4 in Chow already.  \nW4 is completely disconnected from our hand, safe against next player, and not a terminal/honor so no big loss.  \n\nBetter discard: **W4**.\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation:** The hand has good potential for All Types + All Chows (8+ points). W4 is isolated given our existing W1\u2013W3 meld and no other W tiles in hand. Discarding it maintains flexibility to form chows in T and B suits while keeping honors for All Types.  \n\n**Reason:** W4 is not useful for our hand structure, safe against next player who already used W4 in a meld, and keeps our B and T sequences intact for efficient progress toward a winning hand with adequate points.  \n\n**Answer:** Play W4"}
